@meta
id: 13-08
title: The whole word and nothing but
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-anchors
par: 33
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+F, the "Match Whole Word" toggle, replace two by hand
open: invoice.ts

@brief
The local called `total` in `buildInvoice` is being renamed `amountDue`,
and the field it becomes in the returned object takes the same name. Two
edits — the declaration and the shorthand property on the return line.

`/total` gets you nowhere useful: `subtotal`, `totalDiscount` and
`totals` all sit above the declaration, and every one of them contains
`total`. `*` would sort that out, but `*` needs the cursor on the word,
and the word is the thing you are trying to reach.

`*` works by wrapping the word in two anchors. You can type them
yourself.

@keys
| `\<` | the start of a word — a position, like `^` |
| `\>` | the end of a word |
| `/\<total\>` | `total` as a whole word, what `*` would have searched for |
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |
| `n` `.` | next whole-word match, same change |

@hints
Rule out `subtotal` and `totalDiscount` by saying that the word has to
start *and* stop where `total` does.
--
`\<` matches the boundary at the start of a word and `\>` the one at
the end. Neither consumes a character. Wrap the word in them.
--
`/\<total\>` `Enter` skips everything with letters attached and lands
on the declaration. `cwamountDue`, `Esc`, then `n.` for the return
line.

@solution
/\<total\><Enter>  cwamountDue<Esc>  n.  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-nine keystrokes, and the four decoys were never visited.

`\<` and `\>` are word anchors. A word boundary is the point between a
word character and a non-word character — the same definition of
"word" that `w`, `iw` and `*` use: letters, digits and underscore. So
`\<total\>` refuses `subtotal` because `b` and `t` are both word
characters and there is no boundary between them, and it refuses
`totalDiscount` for the same reason at the other end. It accepts
`order.total` and `{ total }` and `total;` because a dot, a space and a
semicolon all make a boundary.

When you press `*`, vim builds exactly this: `\<word\>`, using the word
under the cursor. That is why `*` never matched inside a longer name in
chapter 12. Typing the anchors yourself is what you do when the word is
not under your cursor, or when you want only one side — `\<get` for
"anything starting with get", `Id\>` for "anything ending in Id".

The two anchors are the escaped kind: `\<` not `<`, because a plain `<`
is a common character in code and vim keeps it literal. The chapter has
a lesson coming on why some of these need a backslash and some do not,
and what to do about it.

@start invoice.ts
import { VAT_RATE } from './invoice.constants';
import { Order } from '../order/order.entity';

export type InvoiceLine = {
  sku: string;
  quantity: number;
  subtotal: number;
  discount: number;
};

export function buildInvoice(order: Order, lines: InvoiceLine[]) {
  const subtotal = lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.subtotal, 0);
  const totalDiscount = lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.discount, 0);
  const totals = { subtotal, totalDiscount };
  const vat = Math.round((subtotal - totalDiscount) * VAT_RATE);
  const total = subtotal - totalDiscount + vat;

  return { orderId: order.id, currency: order.currency, totals, vat, total };
}

@expected invoice.ts
import { VAT_RATE } from './invoice.constants';
import { Order } from '../order/order.entity';

export type InvoiceLine = {
  sku: string;
  quantity: number;
  subtotal: number;
  discount: number;
};

export function buildInvoice(order: Order, lines: InvoiceLine[]) {
  const subtotal = lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.subtotal, 0);
  const totalDiscount = lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.discount, 0);
  const totals = { subtotal, totalDiscount };
  const vat = Math.round((subtotal - totalDiscount) * VAT_RATE);
  const amountDue = subtotal - totalDiscount + vat;

  return { orderId: order.id, currency: order.currency, totals, vat, amountDue };
}
